brainfuck is an esoteric programming language made by urban müller as a experiment in 1993. it consists of 8 commands and its turing-complete, despite not being something useful on real practice coding, when building software, for example, and being more a subject used to learn low level code and machine thinking.

it works based on a endless sequence of empty cells with values that go from 0 to 255, where increasing a cell with value 255 by one returns it to 0. you start from the first cell and can freely move your pointer to another cell.

the commands are:
+ = increase the selected cell by one
- = decrease the selected cell by one 
> = move your pointer one cell to the right
< = move your pointer one cell to the left
[ = begin a loop that will finish when the selected cell's value is 0
] = close the loop (its needed, like [-] that will subtract until 0 and then pass to the next command)
, = input, stores the key pressed when the code reaches this point. the code waits until an input is given
. = output, releases the value on the selected cells and prints it.

printing values is based on the ASCII table, and brainfuck turns the value into the correspondent character

for an example, saying 'hi!' (yes, in non capital letters) requires you to print the following values: 104, 105, 33. it would be possible to add + 104 times, but it is slower and not optimized. therefore, i could script it like ++++++++++[>++++++++++<-]>++++. (h). it is basically a multiplication. since 'i' is only one value higher, we can simply do it like ++++++++++[>++++++++++<-]>++++.+.

33 is far from 105, which is the current cell's value. we could simply erase it with [-] and start anew, but its more practical to simply move to the next cell and multiply again.

++++++++++[>++++++++++<-]>++++.+.>++++++++++[>+++<-]>+++.

this code can still be more optimized by multiplying on the same loop, like
++++++++++[>++++++++++>+++<<-]>++++.+.>+++.
which would be a (10 . 10) (10 . 3), with the first ++++ being adding four to a cell and turning into the 'h', and so it goes on.
